# Project
A multichannel e-commerce platform for Indian sellers. Sellers create products once;
the platform lists them on Amazon, Flipkart, Meesho and Ajio, keeps inventory synced
across all channels, reads orders/returns, and shows a dashboard. An internal ops
team onboards sellers onto marketplaces.

# Architecture (modular monolith, pnpm + Turborepo monorepo)
- apps/web: Next.js (App Router) — seller dashboard, ops panel, API routes, webhook receivers
- apps/worker: long-running Node process — BullMQ consumers and schedulers
- packages/db: Drizzle schema, migrations, query helpers
- packages/core: business logic (catalog, inventory ledger, listing state machine)
- packages/connectors: one folder per marketplace, all implementing the shared
  MarketplaceConnector interface
- packages/config: shared tsconfig / eslint
Deployment target: AWS ap-south-1 (ECS Fargate, RDS Postgres, ElastiCache, S3, KMS).

# Hard rules
- TypeScript strict mode everywhere. No `any` unless justified in a comment.
- The web app NEVER calls marketplace APIs inside a request. It enqueues a job; the worker does it.
- Our database is the source of truth for catalog and inventory. Marketplaces are outputs.
- Inventory is an append-only ledger of movements. Never overwrite a stock number.
- Every job must be idempotent (use idempotency keys, e.g. marketplace order ID).
- Money is stored as integer paise. Never floats.
- All timestamps stored in UTC (timestamptz). Display in Asia/Kolkata.
- Never store marketplace passwords. Only OAuth tokens, encrypted, referenced via a secrets layer.
- Never log tokens, secrets, or seller KYC data.
- Validate all external input (API bodies, webhooks, marketplace responses) with Zod.
- Config comes from environment variables, validated with Zod at startup. Keep .env.example updated.
- Business logic lives in packages/core, not in Next.js routes or job handlers.
  Routes and handlers stay thin.

# Conventions
- pnpm only. Use latest stable versions; check before adding a dependency.
- Tests: Vitest. Core logic (ledger, mappings, validation) must have unit tests.
- Named exports, no default exports except where Next.js requires them.
- Small files, clear names. Do not create abstractions until they are needed twice.
- When unsure about a marketplace API's behaviour, leave a TODO and say so. Never invent endpoints.